<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Blitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Blitz was the German bombing campaign against British cities from September 1940 to May 1941. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was bombed on 57 consecutive nights at its start, and ports and industrial centres such as Coventry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Plymouth were heavily hit; more than 40,000 civilians were killed, but the campaign failed to break British morale or war production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-blitz.jpg" alt="The Blitz" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: German Air Force photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinkel_He_111_over_Wapping,_East_London.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the war, writers on air power such as Giulio Douhet and Billy Mitchell held that bombers could not be stopped and that bombing civilians would collapse morale. From July to September 1940 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> attacked Fighter Command and its airfields to gain air superiority as a prelude to invasion. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> still fighting, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and Reichsmarschall Hermann Göring ordered a new policy of mass attacks on British cities on 6 September 1940, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had prepared. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> held nine million people, a fifth of the population, and the Auxiliary Fire Service had 138,000 personnel by July 1939. Civilians had been issued 50 million respirators. Night defences were weak, however: in July 1940 only 1,200 heavy and 549 light anti-aircraft guns stood in the whole country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first raids, from 7 September 1940, were aimed chiefly at the Port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> relied on radio beams such as Knickebein to find targets at night, and the British, alerted by R. V. Jones, learned to jam and bend them. After October the campaign was fought at night. From November 1940 it turned to industrial cities, above all in the West Midlands. The raid on Coventry on the night of 14 November destroyed most of the city centre and its cathedral, damaged about 21 factories seriously and caused a brief 20 percent dip in aircraft production.</p><p>A large raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 29 December 1940 produced a firestorm known as the Second Great Fire of London. In 1941 Hitler, prompted by Erich Raeder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kriegsmarine">Kriegsmarine</a>, ordered attacks on British ports and shipping to support the submarine war. The campaign ended with a large raid on the night of 10–11 May 1941; the last date given for it is 16 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> dropped around 40,000 long tons of bombs, but British production rose steadily; the chief effect was to force the dispersal of aircraft and component manufacture. Most severely hit cities took 10 to 15 days to recover, Birmingham three months. The German command lacked a method for destroying British industry, and its effort was spread across many targets. Between June 1940 and March 1941 it recorded the loss of 2,265 aircraft.</p><p>The image of national solidarity, the Blitz spirit, was questioned by Angus Calder in The Myth of the Blitz (1991), who pointed to divisions of class and anti-social behaviour. After the raids, 750,000 tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> rubble went by rail to make runways on airfields in East Anglia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Blitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>The Blitz</b> killed some 43,000 civilians by May 1941 but failed to break British morale.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
